WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.150
Speaker 1: Today we're going to be speaking
with Dr. John Crystal, who's a psychiatrist

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:10.889
and Yale University professor, maybe best
known for the discovery of the rapid antidepressant

00:00:10.889 --> 00:00:14.040
effect of ketamine in depressed patients.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.180
Dr. Crystal, really great having you on.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:17.220
I appreciate it.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:19.789
Speaker 3: Really pleasure to be here today.

00:00:19.789 --> 00:00:25.690
Speaker 1: So maybe to start with, before
we talk about ketamine specifically, it'd

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:30.160
be great to hear from you a little bit about
depression more generally.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.160
You know, you talk to five different psychiatrists,
you'll often get five, at least slightly different

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:42.739
answers about to what degree is depression,
situational or caused by a chemical or hormonal

00:00:42.739 --> 00:00:47.300
imbalance or some combination or it depends
on the particular case.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.400
What's your sense of our best understanding
about depression at this point?

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.070
Speaker 3: Sure.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.450
So I think there are some things that we can
all agree on.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:59.269
One, that it's far more common than we ever
thought.

00:00:59.269 --> 00:01:05.980
Two, that it has far more detrimental effects
on people's lives than we probably had previously

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:06.980
appreciated.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:07.980
And.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:14.439
And three, that a lot of people who try to
get treatment for depression find that the

00:01:14.439 --> 00:01:18.700
treatment that they start out with isn't giving
them the kind of full response that that they're

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.340
looking for.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:25.189
And as a result, even for many people who
are in treatment, depression continues to

00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:27.369
take a toll on them.

00:01:27.369 --> 00:01:34.470
And I think another thing that we would agree
about depression is that it's really fundamentally

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.369
different than having a bad day.

00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:38.590
Now, we've all had a bad day.

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:43.100
We've all probably had moments of despair,
even.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:51.439
But to sit in that state all day, every day
or most of every day is a totally different

00:01:51.439 --> 00:01:52.689
experience.

00:01:52.689 --> 00:01:57.439
That is hard to appreciate if you haven't
been through it yourself.

00:01:57.439 --> 00:02:06.549
And so when we get to questions of causes,
we have to appreciate that there are enormous.

00:02:06.549 --> 00:02:12.000
Depression is a very broad term and there
are probably many different kinds of depressions.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:19.050
And unfortunately, this is one area where
psychiatry has not really.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.690
Led us to a satisfactory place.

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:25.450
In other words, we appreciate that there are
different types of depression that people

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:33.170
might have, some that are purely a reaction
to bereavement or getting fired or having

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:38.349
your marriage break up and where you have
an issue or a problem that you just can't

00:02:38.349 --> 00:02:44.200
kind of work through in it and in it takes
you down into this really negative place.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:49.480
But there clearly are other people who have
depression that comes on, as you point out,

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:56.019
because of a hormonal imbalance, because of
a medical problem, because of other other

00:02:56.019 --> 00:02:58.700
kinds of things going on in the body.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:04.730
And we're we know a lot more about the interplay
between the body and the brain in relation

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.379
to depression that we than we ever did before.

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:12.850
And one of the things that we've learned is
that medical problems in in the body, heart

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:20.569
disease, asthma, arthritis, all of these things,
even obesity, can contribute to a process

00:03:20.569 --> 00:03:25.590
in the body called inflammation, which when
we think of inflammation in our joint, we

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.010
think of a sort sore joint.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:33.099
When we think of inflammation in the brain,
it affects the way that the circuits are regulated

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:37.060
and can contribute, we think, to depression.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:41.909
But but there is also probably a genetic contribution.

00:03:41.909 --> 00:03:47.010
About 30% or so of the risk for depression
is is genetic.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:51.920
And one of the things that you can see in
some people who don't have a major life stress

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.690
and don't have a medical problem contributing
is that some people out of the blue, just

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:01.790
part of their own make up will will experience
into depression.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:06.200
And that's that's really important in all
of these cases.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:15.180
You know, whether it's primarily stress, primarily
hormonal, primarily a genetic vulnerability,

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:20.650
there's still we're talking about this very
kind of complex interplay of a person and

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:22.210
their surroundings.

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:28.789
And so it's not surprising that we treat depression
sometimes with psychotherapy, sometimes with

00:04:28.789 --> 00:04:31.300
medications, sometimes with both.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:37.039
And other things like getting a lot of social
support, getting good exercise, having a healthy

00:04:37.039 --> 00:04:43.440
diet can also help people be, make get their
best chance of recovering from their depression.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:50.410
Speaker 1: So before we talk about ketamine,
just briefly treating depression with SSRI

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:54.270
arise from the research I did before our interview.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:05:00.199
It seems like it's pretty clear that the SSRI
is work better than placebo.

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:05.650
They don't necessarily work at numbers that
are incredibly overwhelming.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:09.130
It's not 90%, but it does seem better than
placebo.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:13.900
Is that still your understanding and what
exactly are SSRI is doing in the brain?

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:14.900
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:22.740
So so first, absolutely, SSRI is are extremely
helpful for many people.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:29.590
And and so I'd say if if people were just
starting, they're having their first experience

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:36.380
of depression and they get treated with with
an SSRI that they could expect about a 5050

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:40.610
chance of of getting better for that that
SSRI.

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:45.759
But the reality is that that that one of the
things that we've learned about depression

00:05:45.759 --> 00:05:51.330
for many people, perhaps because of the complex
interplay that we are talking about, is that

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:54.200
it can be a chronic or recurring disorder.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:06:00.380
And so what sometimes happens is that people
who maybe in their first episode responded

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:06.530
to the SSRI find that later on the medication
isn't working so well.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:14.880
So how SSRI is work is to raise the level
of serotonin bathing the brain by preventing

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.699
its reuptake after release.

00:06:17.699 --> 00:06:25.660
So it does increase the brain's exposure to
to to to depression.

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:31.849
And then it's the brain's reaction to having
so much serotonin around that evokes adaptive

00:06:31.849 --> 00:06:39.510
changes in the brain that that help the regulation
of mood and other aspects to function better.

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:46.610
And and this process is dependent on the ongoing
availability of serotonin in the brain.

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:52.380
So if you stopped the SSRI abruptly, many
people will find that their depression symptoms

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:53.870
will come back.

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:59.629
Or if you just deplete the body of serotonin,
deplete the deplete the brain of serotonin,

00:06:59.629 --> 00:07:04.420
even though people are staying on the on the
SSRI medications, they may have a transient

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:05.420
relapse.

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:10.389
And that was done for research purposes to
help to understand how these medications work.

00:07:10.389 --> 00:07:12.979
So that's a general introduction to the SSRI.

00:07:12.979 --> 00:07:18.300
Speaker 1: So let's now talk a little bit
about ketamine, maybe what what is.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:23.220
I mean, what were some of the early medical
uses that at least it was tested for?

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:27.220
And then how did this start to become something
that was thought of as maybe effective for

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:28.220
depression?

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:31.820
Speaker 3: Yeah, well, to me, it's a remarkable
story.

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:40.330
So ketamine is a drug that was identified
as an anesthetic medication in the in the

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:41.330
1960s.

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:50.290
And a good friend and colleague, Dr. Ed Domino,
first gave this drug to people and helped

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:56.460
to describe its peculiar effects, which he
and others called dissociative anesthesia,

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:02.259
which is its capacity to produce a kind of
twilight state that there is people sometimes

00:08:02.259 --> 00:08:04.539
called dissociation.

00:08:04.539 --> 00:08:14.159
And the they didn't know how it worked when
they discovered, in fact, the field of neuroscience

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:20.210
really didn't figure out how ketamine worked
until the 1980s.

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:28.840
And then what we what was found out then was
that ketamine works by blocking the action

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:34.789
of glutamate at one of its receptors called
the NMDA subtype of glutamate receptor.

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:38.030
Now, you might say, wait, I've heard of serotonin
before.

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:40.070
I haven't heard of glutamate.

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:41.339
And how is that?

00:08:41.339 --> 00:08:44.320
Why would you think of glutamate for depression?

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:50.020
So this gets back to, in a way, the logic
that carried us to test ketamine first in

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:56.810
depressed patients, which is that for a long
time from, from the first identification of

00:08:56.810 --> 00:09:03.820
these transmitters to I'd say certainly the,
the, the mid to late 1990s, maybe even later,

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:10.149
most people thought of depression as a disorder
of the norepinephrine like noradrenaline or

00:09:10.149 --> 00:09:12.790
serotonin systems of the brain.

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:18.660
These are chemical systems in very primitive
parts of the brain that project to the higher

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:23.050
centers of the brain and tune the activity
of these higher brain centers.

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:26.930
So they reasoned, if serotonin was awry, not
functioning.

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:34.130
Right, maybe if we give we give us a serotonin
reuptake inhibiting drug.

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:39.260
Well, treat the symptoms of the depression
by normalizing serotonin.

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:46.050
Well, research by several groups, including
the work done here at Yale, by my colleague

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:51.510
Dennis Charney and Pedro de Gato, George HENNINGER
and others, they showed that if you deplete

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:55.170
the body of serotonin, you don't produce depression.

00:09:55.170 --> 00:09:58.990
If you deplete the body of norepinephrine,
you don't produce depression.

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:03.399
And that the biology of depression is is is
more complicated.

00:10:03.399 --> 00:10:08.930
In some ways, this was a kind of intellectual
crisis moment for the thinking about the biology

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:15.980
of depression and in the way that Dr. Cherney
and I reasoned our way around this problem

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:23.010
was to say, Well, maybe if depression doesn't
predominantly live in the serotonin or norepinephrine

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:30.790
nerve cells themselves, maybe the depression
lives in the targets of of norepinephrine

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:35.760
and serotonin, the higher brain centers in
the two main brain centers, we thought of

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:41.730
where the cerebral cortex, the highest executive
control of our thought and behavior and emotion,

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:47.730
and the limbic system, the generators or drivers
of emotional experience in the brain.

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:51.330
And and with that came a kind of an aha.

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:54.529
Moment, which is if.

00:10:54.529 --> 00:11:03.220
If depression is a cortical disorder, not
a brainstem disorder, then the intrinsic signaling

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:08.420
mechanisms of these high, higher same brain
centers, the main information highways of

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:13.639
the higher brain centers should be involved
in depression and its treatment.

00:11:13.639 --> 00:11:19.300
And those information high higher information
highways involve the neurotransmitter glutamate

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:25.209
as the main excitatory neurotransmitter of
the cortex, like 90% of the synapses.

00:11:25.209 --> 00:11:30.149
So we thought, let's test a drug that blocks
one of these glutamate receptors.

00:11:30.149 --> 00:11:33.600
The drug we chose to do that was Academy.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:42.350
And what we found was this rapid improvement
in mood within 24 hours, say, of, of a single

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:43.350
dose academy.

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:45.670
And in a large number of the people we tested.

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:49.980
Speaker 1: What is the dosage idea when it
comes to ketamine?

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:52.270
Is it the type of thing that is?

00:11:52.270 --> 00:11:55.660
It is taken daily like an SSRI.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:59.639
Do the effects fall off similarly or differently?

00:11:59.639 --> 00:12:01.600
Like is it how would it be administered?

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:02.920
Speaker 3: Right.

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:09.180
So so TIME magazine called ketamine, the anti
antidepressant.

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:13.589
And and that is because it doesn't work like
anything else we know.

00:12:13.589 --> 00:12:18.899
First, unlike a medication that you give a
little bit of the drug, you don't have much

00:12:18.899 --> 00:12:23.019
effect of the drug and you take it every day
as part of your daily life.

00:12:23.019 --> 00:12:24.810
That's not at all what ketamine is like.

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:31.779
When ketamine is is you give a dose that that
is high enough to be to trigger the antidepressant

00:12:31.779 --> 00:12:33.959
response.

00:12:33.959 --> 00:12:42.519
And and those antidepressant effects last
a week or two, four in people.

00:12:42.519 --> 00:12:46.860
And so people generally start getting two
doses a week.

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:51.730
Then after a month or so, one dose a week,
then every other week.

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:57.300
And so when people are maintained on it, if
they need to be maintained down it, then they

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:01.649
may only get a dose once every three weeks
or once a month.

00:13:01.649 --> 00:13:05.670
And by spreading it out, people don't become
tolerant to it.

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:11.860
They tend to, if anything, to become more
sensitive to the antidepressant effects.

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:17.680
And it turns out in the long term data with
ketamine, that as the version of ketamine

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:25.860
developed by Janssen, that it's very helpful
in preventing relapse of depression and so

00:13:25.860 --> 00:13:32.519
that it's a good long term treatment, as well
as a strategy for producing a rapid antidepressant

00:13:32.519 --> 00:13:38.269
response and to help people who haven't previously
responded to antidepressant medications.

00:13:38.269 --> 00:13:44.399
Speaker 1: Are the doses large enough when
they are given that they have a sort of psychoactive

00:13:44.399 --> 00:13:46.459
or maybe dissociative type effect.

00:13:46.459 --> 00:13:54.779
Speaker 3: When given in the way that we end
both, we in our laboratory with ketamine and

00:13:54.779 --> 00:13:58.950
later Janssen with ketamine give ketamine.

00:13:58.950 --> 00:13:59.950
Yes.

00:13:59.950 --> 00:14:02.279
They tend to produce psychoactive effects.

00:14:02.279 --> 00:14:05.050
They can make people feel spacey.

00:14:05.050 --> 00:14:07.990
They can have altered sensory perception.

00:14:07.990 --> 00:14:14.509
They might experience, for example, that their
arm or your fingers feel longer, or they may

00:14:14.509 --> 00:14:18.029
have a feeling that the room is breathing
in or out.

00:14:18.029 --> 00:14:23.620
And what we find is that if we prepare people
for those kinds of effects, if we're with

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:28.480
them when they are having those experiences,
and then if we can have the chance to talk

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:34.680
about them afterwards, then that people can
both understand what's going on, not find

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:37.850
it very frightening and have overall a good
experience.

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:42.940
Speaker 1: Last thing I wanted to ask about
what's the sort of regulatory and legal status

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:47.089
right now of using ketamine in this way per
the FDA?

00:14:47.089 --> 00:14:48.680
Speaker 3: Yes.

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:55.899
So as ketamine, the isomer of the ketamine
molecule, ketamine comes in two mirror images.

00:14:55.899 --> 00:15:03.190
And so one of the mirror images, the isomer
academy is developed by Janssen and approved

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:09.820
by the FDA for the treatment of treatment
resistant depression and depression in the

00:15:09.820 --> 00:15:13.910
context of suicide risk.

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:19.339
The standard ketamine that's used in anesthesia
is approved by the FDA for anesthesia.

00:15:19.339 --> 00:15:20.339
Right.

00:15:20.339 --> 00:15:28.350
And what that means is that doctors can prescribe
the receiver ketamine for the whatever they

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:33.649
want, but it is not approved by the FDA for
the treatment of depression.

00:15:33.649 --> 00:15:38.089
And that means that doctors who are prescribing
this form of ketamine have some additional

00:15:38.089 --> 00:15:41.850
liability because it's not endorsed by the
FDA.

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:47.569
Also, the consequence of it not being approved
by the FDA is that some insurance companies

00:15:47.569 --> 00:15:56.860
only cover the Esketamine version, that the
Esketamine version is given as a nasal spray.

00:15:56.860 --> 00:16:01.160
And people take a certain number of puffs
of the nasal spray that's appropriate for

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:05.759
them and worked out with their doctor.

00:16:05.759 --> 00:16:11.149
And and that version of of Academy, that version
of ketamine.

00:16:11.149 --> 00:16:18.250
The ESKETAMINE is FDA approved and and increasingly
covered by insurance and Medicare and Medicaid.

00:16:18.250 --> 00:16:23.300
Speaker 1: We have been speaking with Dr.
John Crystal, who is a psychiatrist and Yale

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:25.700
University professor.

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:26.899
Really interesting.

00:16:26.899 --> 00:16:30.830
And we look forward to reading more about
these these uses.

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:32.339
Really appreciate your time today.

00:16:32.339 --> 00:16:34.019
Speaker 3: It's wonderful.

00:16:34.019 --> 00:16:36.980
Wonderful to be here and to talk about this
area.

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:37.980
So thanks for having me on.

